/>

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 closed on 5 October with two outcomes: the parser is fixed, and the booking-v3 form is retired. The
one-day move was in neither. Its ticket stays open.
</Callout>

## Since #4471 closed

Engineering's update of 5 October covers two of the three problems:

- `parser`: fixed, `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`. [Ticket #1]({repo}/issues/1) closed as done.
- `hint`: won't fix, because the booking-v3 form is retired. No new reports can come in this way.
  [Ticket #2]({repo}/issues/2) closed as not planned.
- `timing`: not in the update. [Ticket #3]({repo}/issues/3) stays open: a parser fix does not move a day
  back. A new report about a day off by one goes there, not to a closed ticket.

## The problems

**`parser`, [ticket #1]({repo}/issues/1).** The shared parser reads dates month first, and every tenant in
#4471 writes the day first. "Our 7 April reminder went out on 4 July." Every channel, every tenant.

**`hint`, [ticket #2]({repo}/issues/2).** The booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field.
People follow the hint, and the form reads what they typed the tenant's way, day first. Only the form, only
booking-v3 tenants. It looks like the parser bug, and a parser fix leaves it as it is.

**`timing`, [ticket #3]({repo}/issues/3).** No month flipped: "Invite says Tuesday 12 May. Calendar says
Monday 11 May." "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday." Most likely a date converted
between time zones. Not reproduced yet; the ticket asks the reporters for times and zones.

## How the reports were counted

The desk's own way, as `CauseTable` counts on the #4471 page:

1. One report per id: the export pages overlap, so some reports come twice.
2. Each report under its cause, by tenant and channel (`causes.ts`): a form report at a booking-v3 tenant is
   `hint`, any other report from a day-first tenant is `parser`.
3. Reports the desk read as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`) stay counted where they are filed and are
   counted again as `timing`, their own problem.

The intake tag is not used. It guesses from the reporter's browser, and for these reports it is wrong
often enough to split them the wrong way.

## Why one ticket held three

The desk merges on subject alone ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)). All three
problems arrive as "wrong day on the invite", so they met in one ticket. The #4471 page shows the split
already: its table puts the `hint` reports in a row of their own, and a footnote says the one-day moves
are counted under `parser`. Its headline still says one issue.

